 2  A
 SO2 + photon = SO + O
 SO2 + photon = S + O2
!
! total photoabsorption cross section in the range   4.769-351.745 nm from Feng et al 1999, Chem Phys, 240, 371
!               updated cross section in the range 106.100-403.700 nm from Manatt et al 1993, J Quant Spectr Radiat Transfer, 50, 267
!               updated cross section in the range  40.015-100.400 nm from Holland et al 1995, Chem Phys, 201, 299
!               updated cross section in the range 171.770-237.000 nm from Wu et al 2000, Icarus, 145, 289
!
! photoionization       cross section in the range  40.015-101.500 nm from Holland et al 1995, Chem Phys, 201, 299
! photoionization       yield         in the range   4.769- 40.015 nm assumed to be 1
!
! photoionization threshold is 100.4 nm (Holland et al 1995)
! two photodissociation channels are possible:
! (a) SO2 + photon = SO + O   (lambda < 217.9 nm; Huebner et al 1992)
! (b)              = S + O2   (lambda < 207.0 nm; Huebner et al 1992)
! quantum yields (relative to phodissociation) for (a) and (b) are 0.5 at 184.9 nm (Driscoll & Warneck 1968, J Phys Chem, 72, 3736)
! we assume them to be 0.5 as well up to the dissociation threshold of channel (b)
!
! sigma is the total photoabsorption cross section
! yields are relative to photoabsorption (derived from photoabsorption minus photoionization)
!
! wlth[nm]  sigma[cm2]   yield(s)...
